
Problem 1. Explain, in your own words, the differences and similarities of
interfaces, abstract classes, and classes

- - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - -
A Class is like a car, being its own thing, but you could also have a self-driving car, making that a subclass.
No need for abstraction, since "car" is still its own thing.

    Class Car
    Class Self-Driving-Car extends Car

- - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - -
An abstract class is like making a "vehicle". Here we need to know if it is a car, a bike, a plane or something else.
We need to abstract it here.

    abstract class Vehicle { /* speed, abstract int getWheelCount() */ }
    class Car extends Vehicle { ... }
    class Bike extends Vehicle { ... }
    class Plane extends Vehicle { ... }

- - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - -
An interface is used when a capability cuts across the hierarchy,
meaning it applies to types that don’t share a common parent.
Think about abilities that only some of them have:
Refuelable:              a car and a plane can refuel, a bike can’t.
Electric / Chargeable:   some cars and some bikes, but not a plane (for now).
Flyable:                 a plane, but also a helicopter, and also things that aren’t vehicles at all, like a Bird or a Drone.

    interface Refuelable { void refuel(double liters); }
    interface Chargeable { void charge(int minutes); }

    class Car extends Vehicle implements Refuelable { ... }
    class ElectricBike extends Vehicle implements Chargeable { ... }
    class HybridCar extends Vehicle implements Refuelable, Chargeable { ... }

- - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - -
_____________________________________________________________________________________________
A class is a complete blueprint: it defines state (fields)
and behavior (methods with bodies), and you can create objects from it directly.

An abstract class is an incomplete blueprint.
It captures what a family of related types has in common (shared state and shared behavior),
but leaves some methods unimplemented because they only make sense for a concrete subtype.
You can’t instantiate it.

An interface is a pure contract: it says
“any type that claims to be this must be able to do these things”
without saying how, and (traditionally) without holding any state.
